<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nathdwara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nathdwara is a town in the Rajsamand district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, about 45 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, which grew around the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a>. The image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> was brought there in 1672 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> region, to protect it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s hostility, under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raj-singh-i">Raj Singh I</a>. The temple is the chief shrine of the Pushtimarg tradition, and the town is known for its pichhwai temple paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nathdwara.jpg" alt="Nathdwara" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kunwar Nadaan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishwas_Swaroopam_after_completion_of_work.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nathdwara lies in the Aravalli hills on the banks of the Banas River, in the Rajsamand district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Its name means Gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a>. Shrinathji is a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> resembling his incarnation as a child of seven. According to the temple's account, the deity was first worshipped at Jatpura near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and was moved in 1672 from the Govardhan hill, then kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> for almost six months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The shrine was built in the 17th century on the spot that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a> himself chose, according to tradition. The image was being carried to protect it from the Mughal ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, and at the village of Sihad the wheels of the bullock cart sank axle-deep in mud and could not be moved. The accompanying priests took this as the Lord's chosen place, and a temple was built there under the rule and protection of Maharana Raj Singh of Mewar. It is also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haveli">Haveli</a> of Shrinathji.</p><p>Nathdwara is a major shrine of the Pushti Marg, also called the Vallabha Sampradaya or Shuddha Advaita, founded by Vallabhacharya. His son Vitthal Nathji organised the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a> there, and the royal family of Nathdwara, called Tilkayat, belongs to the lineage of Vallabhacharya. The image of Naveet Priya came with Shrinathji, and Madan Mohan was given to Shrinathji by the wife of Maharana Fateh Singh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> and Mewar. Both temples share the administration of the Shrinathji Temple. The image of Vithal Nath, the second of the seven svarups of the Pushti Marg, has a separate administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave Nathdwara a population of 42,016. Artists working near the temple are known for pichwai paintings of the Mewar school, which centre on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a>, shown as a black-faced figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> holding up Mount Govardhan, and they also paint small works on paper, mostly on themes from the legend of Krishna. A statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> called the Statue of Belief, 369 feet tall, opened at Nathdwara on 29 October 2022. Other temples in the town honour Khera Mata, Mahadeva and Hanuman, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> have a small presence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nathdwara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1672 the image of Shrinathji, a form of Krishna, was brought to <b>Nathdwara</b> in Mewar, which became a major pilgrimage centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
